WEBVTT

c01
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:07.850
Picture a team that is stuck with a slow, painful process.

c02
00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:13.150
Then a faster technology arrives, and they put it right on top of that process,

c03
00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:14.990
exactly as it is.

c04
00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:16.930
Does the problem go away?

c05
00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:20.410
No, it doesn't. It gets locked in.

c06
00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:24.830
Now it just runs faster, and at a bigger scale.

c07
00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:32.730
Back in 1990, Harvard Business Review published an article called Reengineering Work.

c08
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:34.410
Its message was blunt.

c09
00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:36.730
Don't automate. Obliterate.

c10
00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:39.950
In other words, don't speed up the old process.

c11
00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:42.440
Wipe it out, and start again.

c12
00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:50.250
The idea is simple. If you put new technology on a broken process without taking it apart first,

c13
00:00:50.270 --> 00:00:53.640
you only speed up the inefficiency you already have.

c14
00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:58.960
The article was about ordinary computing, but it applies to AI just as well.

c15
00:00:59.330 --> 00:01:02.990
Let's see it in two real cases, decades apart.

c16
00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:10.600
Case one takes place in the early 1980s, in the accounts payable department of a U.S. car company.

c17
00:01:11.110 --> 00:01:12.780
500 people work there.

c18
00:01:12.850 --> 00:01:16.480
A Japanese company runs the same department with about 100.

c19
00:01:16.690 --> 00:01:21.060
That's five times fewer, and the U.S. company wants to close that gap.

c20
00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:25.890
So, before you see what they found, make the first call yourself.

c21
00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:29.770
So, what did they find?

c22
00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:32.950
Mapping the process showed the real reason.

c23
00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:38.640
Most of the work went into one thing, which was matching three separate documents by hand.

c24
00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:43.090
Faster systems for the old process didn't close the gap.

c25
00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:46.330
Instead, they rewrote the process itself.

c26
00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:49.900
Now, payment is triggered straight from the receipt record,

c27
00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:52.230
and there is no separate invoice.

c28
00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:57.920
According to reports, the headcount needed fell by about 75%.

c29
00:01:59.230 --> 00:02:04.350
Case two is a Swedish fintech, between 2024 and 2025,

c30
00:02:04.370 --> 00:02:06.100
according to public reports.

c31
00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:09.880
An AI agent starts handling its customer conversations,

c32
00:02:10.150 --> 00:02:13.380
about 2.3 million of them every month.

c33
00:02:13.670 --> 00:02:18.940
Reports put the drop in service load at the scale of about 700 service reps.

c34
00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:23.580
And handling time falls from about 11 minutes to under two.

c35
00:02:24.590 --> 00:02:28.620
These figures come from company publications and media reports.

c36
00:02:28.790 --> 00:02:33.400
They're here for learning, and not as proof that people should be replaced.

c37
00:02:34.570 --> 00:02:40.080
Then, by early 2025, the company starts hiring human service reps again.

c38
00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:41.940
Why would they do that?

c39
00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:46.260
On simple requests, the AI did about as well as people.

c40
00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:51.240
But on complex ones, like disputes or personally difficult cases,

c41
00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:53.480
quality dropped noticeably.

c42
00:02:54.490 --> 00:02:55.740
So, what went wrong?

c43
00:02:56.190 --> 00:02:57.750
Decide before you hear it.

c44
00:02:59.930 --> 00:03:05.200
Management admitted that focusing too much on efficiency had hurt service quality.

c45
00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:09.090
The fix was a hybrid model, where AI handles the routine,

c46
00:03:09.110 --> 00:03:13.280
and a person steps in when empathy and judgment are needed.

c47
00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:18.690
Two cases, decades apart, with completely different technology,

c48
00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:20.390
teach the same lesson.

c49
00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:23.730
Process mapping is not a bureaucratic step.

c50
00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:29.220
It's what separates a change that works from a change you have to fix after the fact.

c51
00:03:30.330 --> 00:03:34.680
And notice that both stories are told in numbers, in headcount,

c52
00:03:34.770 --> 00:03:37.160
in handling time, and in quality.

c53
00:03:37.530 --> 00:03:41.080
Without a number, nobody can check a change.
